**Approach.** Build a **bounded blocking queue** (producer-consumer buffer). Producers block when it is full, the consumer blocks when it is empty, and all access is guarded by a lock.

```java
class BlockingBuffer<T> {
    private final Object[] items;
    private int head, tail, count;
    private final ReentrantLock lock = new ReentrantLock();
    private final Condition notFull = lock.newCondition();
    private final Condition notEmpty = lock.newCondition();

    BlockingBuffer(int cap) { items = new Object[cap]; }

    void put(T x) throws InterruptedException {
        lock.lock();
        try {
            while (count == items.length) notFull.await();
            items[tail] = x; tail = (tail + 1) % items.length; count++;
            notEmpty.signal();
        } finally { lock.unlock(); }
    }

    @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
    T take() throws InterruptedException {
        lock.lock();
        try {
            while (count == 0) notEmpty.await();
            T x = (T) items[head]; items[head] = null;
            head = (head + 1) % items.length; count--;
            notFull.signal();
            return x;
        } finally { lock.unlock(); }
    }
}
```

**Driver.** Start two threads that each call `put` in a loop (for example 1..N tagged with the producer id) and one thread that calls `take` in a loop. Stop with a **poison pill** value per producer, or a count of expected items.

**Why it is correct**
- The lock gives **mutual exclusion**, and releasing/acquiring it provides memory visibility.
- `await` releases the lock while waiting. The `while` loop re-checks the condition after waking, which handles **spurious wakeups** and the case where another thread consumed the slot first.
- Separate conditions mean producers wake only consumers and vice versa.

**In production** just use `ArrayBlockingQueue` or `LinkedBlockingQueue`, which do exactly this; mention it so the interviewer knows you know the library. With a single consumer, ordering per producer is preserved, but the interleaving between producers is nondeterministic.

**Pitfalls:** using `if` instead of `while`, signalling without holding the lock, calling `await` outside the lock, and an unbounded queue hiding a slow consumer until memory runs out.
